   "title": "West Bengal election is not legally valid: Former CEC S.Y. Quraishi",
   "url": "https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/west-bengal/west-bengal-election-is-not-legally-validformer-cec-sy-quraishi/article71674557.ece",
   "domain": "thehindu.com",
   "source": "thehindu.com",
   "published": "Sat, 07 Nov 2026 15:39:47 +0530",
   "_dt": 1794046187.0,
   "ageDays": 0.07,
   "summary": "Quraishi alleged that the West Bengal elections were not legally valid. “Were the West Bengal elections legally valid? This comes after the former CEC raised allegations against the SIR process and discrepancies at the West Bengal elections while delivering the keynote address at the Hashim Abdul Halim Memorial Lecture on Thursday (November 5, 2026) in Kolkata.",
   "summary_zh": "库赖什指称，西孟加拉邦选举在法律上并不有效。“西孟加拉邦的选举在法律上是否有效？”此前，前首席选举官在2026年11月5日星期四于加尔各答举行的哈希姆·阿卜杜勒·哈利姆纪念讲座上发表主旨讲话时，曾对特别密集修订（SIR）程序以及西孟加拉邦选举中存在的差异提出指控。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Former Chief Election Commissioner S.Y. Quraishi alleged that the West Bengal elections were not legally valid. He reinstated that the decisions at the Election Commission of India should be based on a majority. “Were the West Bengal elections legally valid? In my opinion, it was not. I have said this several times; I will say it again. Now it is in front of the Supreme Court; I am hoping they will take the right decision,” Mr. Quraishi said. He further added that some parts of it may have been valid but also said that in places where there was suspicious of majority Muslim voters being removed must be rechecked and go for re-polls if needed. “This government has been in power for 6-7 months. People are happy. But some people are raising questions, maybe because they did not get the outcome that they desired,” Minister Dilip Ghosh said on Friday (November 6, 2026), reacting to Mr. Quraishi’s allegations. He also warned that accepting the people’s verdict and choice of government is the right decision; otherwise the people opposing it have a “tough” road ahead. This comes after the former CEC raised allegations against the SIR process and discrepancies at the West Bengal elections while delivering the keynote address at the Hashim Abdul Halim Memorial Lecture on Thursday (November 5, 2026) in Kolkata. “The CEC is not the Election Commission. The rules first call for unanimous decisions. If that is not possible, there must be a majority-based decision among the three members of the ECI,” Mr. Quraishi said. He added that the CEC is not the ECI on their own, and their decision alone cannot be final. The former CEC said this in reference to The Indian Express report, which had claimed that election commissioners Sukhbir Singh Sandhu and Vivek Joshi had raised at least 14 objections to the various decisions allegedly taken unilaterally by CEC Gyanesh Kumar. Meanwhile, Mr. Quraishi also said that the voter is not supposed to beg for their voting rights; rather, tradition in the past ensured that voter cards were delivered to the homes of each voter, and they did not have to negotiate to be included in the voter lists. He accused the special intensive revision (SIR) was used as a process to do backdoor checks for citizenships. “It was neither smooth nor transparent. So many questions were raised that are currently being heard in the Supreme Court. Now, things will come out clearly once their judgment arrives, regarding what went wrong and what was correct,” Mr. Quraishi added. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
