   "title": "US Midterm Elections: Gen Z, Gay, Muslim, Other Candidates Who Made History In Landmark Wins",
   "url": "https://leadership.ng/us-midterm-elections-gen-z-gay-muslim-other-candidates-who-made-history-in-landmark-wins/",
   "domain": "leadership.ng",
   "source": "Leadership（尼日利亚）",
   "published": "Wed, 04 Nov 2026 11:55:46 +0000",
   "_dt": 1793793346.0,
   "ageDays": 3.0,
   "summary": "The elections also produced the country’s first Black woman governor and additional milestones in gubernatorial representation, with candidates from both the Democratic and Republican parties making history. Democrat Abdul El-Sayed made history in Michigan after defeating Republican Mike Rogers to become the first Muslim elected to the United States Senate. Democrat Chris Pappas made history after winning the Senate race in New Hampshire, becoming the first openly gay man elected to the United States Senate.",
   "summary_zh": "此次选举还产生了该国首位黑人女性州长，并推动州长代表性取得更多突破，民主党和共和党的候选人均创造了历史。民主党人Abdul El-Sayed在密歇根州击败共和党人Mike Rogers，成为首位当选美国参议院的穆斯林。民主党人Chris Pappas赢得新罕布什尔州参议院选举后创造历史，成为首位当选美国参议院的公开同性恋男性。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "The elections also produced the country’s first Black woman governor and additional milestones in gubernatorial representation, with candidates from both the Democratic and Republican parties making history. Democrat Abdul El-Sayed made history in Michigan after defeating Republican Mike Rogers to become the first Muslim elected to the United States Senate. His victory also prevented Republicans from flipping the seat previously held by retiring Democratic Senator Gary Peters, strengthening the Democratic Party’s position in the chamber. El-Sayed’s election represents a significant milestone for Muslim representation in American national politics, particularly in a country where no Muslim had previously served in the Senate. Democrat Peggy Flanagan secured a historic victory in Minnesota, becoming the first Native American woman elected to the United States Senate. Flanagan, who previously served two terms as Minnesota’s lieutenant governor, defeated Republican candidate Michele Tafoya, a former National Football League (NFL) sideline reporter and conservative podcaster. Her victory marks a major milestone for Indigenous representation in the upper chamber of Congress. Democrat Chris Pappas made history after winning the Senate race in New Hampshire, becoming the first openly gay man elected to the United States Senate. His victory marks another milestone for LGBTQ representation in American politics, expanding the diversity of voices represented in the country’s highest legislative chamber. At 29, Democrat Melat Kiros made history after winning the congressional race for Colorado’s 1st District. She becomes the first Generation Z woman elected to the US House of Representatives, marking a significant milestone for younger political representation. Her victory also highlights the growing presence of younger candidates in American electoral politics, as a new generation seeks greater representation in national decision-making. Democrat Keisha Bottoms made history after winning Georgia’s gubernatorial election, becoming the first Black woman elected governor in United States history. The former Atlanta mayor defeated Republican billionaire Rick Jackson to secure the landmark victory. Her election also makes her Georgia’s first Black governor, breaking a longstanding racial barrier in the state’s political leadership. Former US Interior Secretary Deb Haaland secured another historic victory after winning the New Mexico gubernatorial election. The Democrat becomes the first Native American woman elected governor in the United States, defeating Republican Gregg Hull, a former mayor. Haaland previously made history in 2021 when she became the first Native American to serve as US interior secretary under former President Joe Biden. Her gubernatorial victory further expands Indigenous representation in American political leadership. The gubernatorial elections also produced historic victories for Black candidates from both major political parties. Republican Byron Donalds won Florida’s gubernatorial election, becoming the state’s first Black governor. Meanwhile, Democrat David Crowley made history in Wisconsin after winning the governorship, becoming the state’s first Black governor. Their victories represent significant milestones for Black political representation, particularly as the United States has historically had very few Black governors. Maryland Governor Wes Moore, the country’s third elected Black governor and the only one serving at the time, also secured re-election, extending his place in American political history. We’ve got the edge. Get real-time reports, breaking scoops, and exclusive angles delivered straight to your phone. Don’t settle for stale news. Join LEADERSHIP NEWS on WhatsApp for 24/7 updates →",
